Among the synthesized MOFs for sensing purpose, luminescence-functionalized MOFs have attracted more and more attention for they may exhibit various and tunable optical properties and thus have promising applications as a new type of optical sensing materials.9 Usually, most unfunctionalized MOFs can have intrinsic luminescence activities due to using aromatic linkers or lanthanide ions. However, their luminescence activities are not satisfying since most aromatic linkers do not contain large conjugated systems (i.e., fused aromatic rings)
or metal-linker charge transfers are not significant (except for lanthanides-linkers), resulting in low detection sensitivity and thus limiting their sensing applications.
Several efforts have been made to improve luminescence activities of MOFs by functionalizing MOFs, including the utilities of special linkers with high luminescence quantum yield,9 and encapsulation luminescent guest molecules or nanoparticles.10−12 The introduction of highly luminescent materials into MOFs while basically maintaining the original structures and properties of host MOFs has shown obvious improvements in both sensing selectivity and sensitivity. For example, encapsulation of highly fluorescent ${\mathrm{(FL,}}$ quantum yield $>40\%$ ) carbon quantum dots in ZIF-8 has led to a significant increase (2 orders of magnitude improvement) in detection sensitivity for copper ions, compared with fluorescence sensor without MOFs.12 However, there is still much room for improving the sensitivities of MOFs-based sensors. Up until now, most reported MOF sensors are based on luminescence quenching of lanthanide ions or aromatic fluorophores. The luminescence quenching (or signal off) transduction modes do not result in very low detection limits due to significant luminescence backgrounds. Additionally, the luminescence quenching events usually happen between one luminophore and one target, i.e., one point to one point interaction, thus it is difficult to amplify signal by raising the quantities of luminophores in MOFs. Therefore, design and synthesis luminescent MOFs that can be used for signal-on and signal-amplifying sensing of targets will be an important alternative for improving the sensitivities of MOF-based sensors.
As well-known, MOFs are porous materials with very high specific surface areas, which enable them to contain large quantities of cargoes, such as drugs or luminophores. Apparently, the detection sensitivities of MOFs-based sensors would be greatly improved if large quantities of luminophores loaded in MOFs can be responsively released by small quantities of targets in an appropriate way. To reach this target-responsive release of luminophores from MOFs, the luminescence-functionalized MOFs should have following two properties: first, MOFs containing luminophores can maintain great stability in aqueous solutions in the absence of targets in order to get low optical background. Second, the release of large quantities of luminophores should be completed in a short time to obtain high sensitivity and fast response. Apparently, this is a challenging task in the design and synthesis of the desired MOFs since the good stability of luminescencefunctionalized MOFs may conflict with easy release of guest luminophores from MOFs. Generally, if stable MOFs are used, it is difficult to release guest cargoes (luminophores or drugs) from the frameworks in short time. For example, MOFs have now been used for drug delivery and release, in which the drugs are slowly released over tens of hours, even several days. Apparently, these types of MOFs with slow guest material release are unfit for sensing although they have shown promising applications in medicine.
Recently, we have found a kind of tris(bipyridine)ruthenium(II)-functionalized MOFs (RuMOFs) can serve as ideal signalon and signal-amplifying sensing materials. Herein, we demonstrate the synthesis, properties, and sensing applications of the RuMOFs. As shown in Scheme 1, the RuMOFs are assembled from adenine, zinc(II) salt, biphenyldicarboxylate (BPDC), and tris(bipyridine)ruthenium $(\mathrm{II})$ $\left(\operatorname{Ru}({\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}\right)$ . It should be noted here, $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)}}_{3}^{2+}$ was selected as the guest molecules in MOFs since it is a versatile molecule that has many applications in electrochemiluminescence (ECL) sensing,13 biodiagnostics,14 photovoltaics, and organic light-emitting diode.15,16 The main skeleton of the RuMOFs is bio-MOFs$1,^{17-20}$ while the luminescent $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)}}_{3}^{2+}$ is both doped in the frameworks (displacing BPDC) and encapsulated in the pores of MOFs. The luminescence-functionalized RuMOFs are very stable in aqueous solution but can be ion-selectively, rapidly, and completely disassembled by small quantities of mercury ions $(\mathrm{Hg}^{2+})$ , resulting in the release of large quantities of luminescent guest materials, $\operatorname{Ru}({\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ cations, that give rise to strong fluorescence or electrochemiluminescence signals. On the basis of the novel RuMOF material, an ultrasensitive and highly selective chemical sensor for $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ , a highly toxic heavy metal ion often requiring assa y,21−23 has been developed, with a limit of detection (LOD) of $\dot{5}.3\times10^{-13}\mathrm{~M}.$ For the first time, stable luminescence-functionalized MOFs in aqueous solution can be target-responsively disassembled and release guest materials. It is envisioned that the RuMOFs and similar targetresponsive functional MOF materials would have promising applications in ultrasensitive and highly selective chemical sensing and even in accurately controllable drug delivering and releasing.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Chemicals and Materials. Tris (2, 2-bipyridyl) dichlororuthenium(II) hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ ${>}98\%\dot{}$ ) and adenine $(>99\%)$ were purchased from Alfa Aesar. $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -Biphenyldicarboxylate (PBDC, ${>}97\%$ ) was obtained from Sigma-Aldrich. $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $^{N,N}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) of analytical purity were from Tianjin Standard Science and Technology Co. (Tianjin, China). Other chemicals were of reagent grade unless otherwise noted. All chemicals were used without further purification.
Apparatus. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was performed on a TG209F1 thermal analysis system. All TGA experiments were performed under $\Nu_{2}$ atmosphere at temperature over $30{-}800\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with a temperature rising rate of $10\ {}^{\circ}{\bf C}/$ min, and data were analyzed using TA Universal Analysis software package. X-ray powder diffraction patterns were taken using a Rigaku Miniflex X-ray diffractometer at $30~\mathrm{kV}_{;}$ $15~\mathrm{mA}$ for $\operatorname{Cu}\operatorname{K}\alpha$ $\mathit{\Omega}\left(\lambda=1.5406\mathrm{\AA}\right)$ with scan speed of $0.1\ s/$ step and a step size of $0.01^{\circ}$ . TEM and SEM images were taken by a Tecnai G2 F20S-TWIN electronic microscope and a Nova NanoSEM 230 field-emission microscope, respectively. The specific surface areas of the products were measured by the Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) method using nitrogen gas adsorption/desorption at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ (BET, Micromeritics ASAP 2020). Cross-sectional confocal microscopic images were recorded on a laser confocal fluorescence microscope (Nikon C2). Fluorescence spectra were recorded on an F-4600 FL spectrofluorometer. The solid fluorescence was measured on a steady state and transient state fluorescence spectrometer (FLS920). ECL measurements were carried out on an EC&ECL detection system (MPI-E, Remex Electronic Instrument Lt. Co., Xi’an, China) equipped with a glassy carbon (GC) working electrode $2.0\ \mathrm{mm}$ in diameter), a $\mathrm{\Ag/AgCl}$ (3 M KCl) reference electrode, and a Pt wire counter electrode. In ECL measurements, TPrA $\mathrm{(1.0\times10^{-3}~m o l/L)}$ was used as the coreactant, and the potential was cycled within the window from $0\mathrm{V}$ to $+1.25\mathrm{V}_{;}$ with a scan rate of $200~\mathrm{mV/s}$ . An Agilent 7500 ICPMS system (Agilent Technologies, Santa Clara, CA) was used to detect the ${\bar{\mathrm{H}}}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ concentration of the Min River water sample.
Synthesis of ${\mathsf{R u}}({\mathsf{b}}{\mathsf{p}}{\mathsf{y}})_{3}^{\ 2+}$ -Functionalized bio-MOF-1 (RuMOFs). Typically, RuMOFs were prepared by adding adenine $\left(0.25\ \mathrm{mmol}\right),$ , $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyl dicarboxylic acid (BPDC) $\left(0.5\mathrm{\mmol}\right)$ , zinc nitrate hexahydrate $\left(0.75\mathrm{\mmol}\right)$ , tris $(2,2^{\prime}.$ - bipyridine) ruthenium(II) chloride hexahydrate $\left(0.0145\mathrm{mmol}\right)$ ) in a mixed solvent of nitric acid $\left(2\mathrm{mmol}\right)$ , DMF $(27{\mathrm{~mL}})$ , and water $(2\mathrm{mL})$ . The mixed solution was heated to $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 24 ${\mathrm{h}},$ producing rod-shaped crystals. The crystals were collected, washed three times in turn with DMF and water, and finally dried by a vacuum oven at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize2h}$ . The content of $\operatorname{Ru}({\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ in RuMOFs was controlled by adding various concentrations of $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)}}_{3}^{2+}$ (from 0 to $1.25\mathrm{\mM},$ ) in the synthesis solution and calculated from the amount of $\dot{\mathrm{Ru}}(\ensuremath{\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ completely released from a certain weight of RuMOFs into pure water by $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ (Figure S1 in the Supporting Information). A constant content $(3.64~\mathrm{wt}~\%)$ of $\mathrm{{Ru}}(\mathrm{{b}}\bar{\mathrm{{py}}})_{3}^{~2+}$ in RuMOFs was reached when the concentration of $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)}}_{3}^{2+}$ in the synthesis solution was $0.5\mathrm{mM}$ . Therefore, unless otherwise statement, RuMOFs were synthesized in DMF solution containing $0.5\mathrm{\mM\Ru(bpy)_{3}}^{2+}$ .
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Characterization of RuMOFs. The prepared RuMOF crystals are porous (see SEM images in Figure S2 in the Supporting Information) and rod-shaped with length less than $100\ \mu\mathrm{{m}}$ and diameter less than $20\ \mu\mathrm{m}$ (see Figure 1A and Figure S2 in the Supporting Information). Under the excitation of UV or blue light, the whole RuMOFs rod emits strong red fluorescence (Figure 1B). The fluorescence spectrum measurements show that the RuMOFs have a maximum excitation wavelength and a maximum emission wavelength at 455 and $612\ \mathrm{nm},$ , respectively (Figure 1C). The fluorescence spectra of the RuMOFs are consistent with those of pure $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)}}_{3}^{2+}$ ,13,14 indicating the MOFs have been well functionalized with $\mathrm{{Ru}}(\mathrm{{bpy}})_{3}^{-2+}$ . In order to confirm whether the luminopore $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)}}_{3}{}^{2+}$ is homogeneously dispersed in the MOFs or just absorbed on the outmost surface, confocal microscopy was measured for the RuMOFs. The cross-sectional confocal microscopic images (Figure 1D) show that the red fluorescence emits from both the surface and inside of the MOF crystal, suggesting that $\operatorname{Ru}({\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ is indeed dispersed well in whole MOFs.
The comparison of X-ray powder diffraction data of RuMOFs (see red line in Figure 1E) with that of their precursor MOFs (i.e., bio-MOF-1) (see black line in Figure 1E) indicates that the functionalization with $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)}}_{3}^{2+}$ does not impact the crystalline integrity of bio-MOF-1. The BET isotherm of the RuMOFs (Figure 1F) shows a typical type-I isotherm characteristic of a microporous material, from which the BET surface area of the RuMOFs is found to be $424~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},$ which is obviously lower than that the unfunctionalized MOFs $\left(1388\mathrm{~\m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},\right.$ Figure S3 in the Supporting Information). Additionally, TGA data (Figure S4 in the Supporting Information) show that the thermal degradation process of RuMOFs is very similar to bio-MOF-1. There is a weight loss of $5.82\%$ from room temperature to $125^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ which can be attributed to the loss of solvent guest molecules. Thereafter, a small weight loss $2.17\%$ at $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) prior to decomposition, which corresponds to the loss of dimethylammonium cations. Apparently, thermal stability of MOF does not change upon the functionalization with $\mathrm{{Ru}}(\dot{\mathrm{{bpy}}})_{3}^{~2+}$ .
Ion-Selective Triggered Disassembly and Release. It is well-known that most MOFs can strongly adsorb some metal ions; however, unlike the classic MOFs, the RuMOF can be selectively decomposed by $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ ions and release the luminorphore of $\bar{\mathrm{Ru}}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}$ inside. As shown in Figure 2, the interactions between the RuMOFs and 17 commonly existing $\mathrm{H}^{+}$ and metal ions, including $\mathrm{Hg^{2+},L i^{+},N a^{+},K^{+},N H_{4}^{+}}$ $\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ , $\bar{\mathbf{C}}\mathsf{a}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ba}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{\dot{M}n}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ were investigated. After adding $100~\mu\mathbf{M}$ of above cations into water containing the same amount of the RuMOFs, only $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ could trigger the release of the yellow component (i.e., $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)}}_{3}^{2+})$ into the supernatant, whereas no release from the RuMOFs was found in pure water, acid solution (containing $\mathbf{H}^{+}\mathbf{\Sigma},$ ), or solutions of other metal ions. In addition to metal ions, the effects of some commonly existing anions, such as $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ , ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}^{-}$ , ${\mathrm{SO}_{4}}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{CO}_{3}^{2-}.$ , $\mathrm{PO_{4}}^{3-}$ , $\dot{\mathrm{C}}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}{}^{2-};$ , $\bar{\mathrm{CH}}_{3}\mathrm{COO}^{-}$ , ${\mathrm{SO}}_{3}^{2-}$ , $S^{2-}{}_{;}$ , $\mathrm{CN^{-}}$ (the counterions were all ${{\bf{N}}_{}}{{\bf{a}}_{}}^{+}$ ) on the stability of RuMOFs were also investigated. No anion caused the release of $\operatorname{Ru}({\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ from the MOFs (Figure S5 in the Supporting Information). Moreover, the interaction between methylmercury ion $\left(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{Hg}^{+}\right)$ , an important organic mercury in environment, and RuMOFs was evaluated and compared with the inorganic of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ (Figure S6 in the Supporting Information). It is evident that the organic mercury, $\mathrm{\bar{CH}_{3}H g^{+}}$ , does not cause any release of $\mathrm{{Ru}}(\mathrm{{bpy}})_{3}^{~2+}$ from the RuMOFs. These experimental results show an extremely selective interaction between $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ and the RuMOFs.
The interaction between the RuMOFs and $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ ion was further investigated in more detail. Before interaction with $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}.$ , the RuMOFs are a kind of dark yellow powder (left cuvette in Figure 3A) and emit very bright red light under UV light (left cuvette in Figure 3B). The RuMOFs powder can remain very stable in water without $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ for several months. However, after interaction with $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ ions into water, yellow species can be released completely into the water phase (right cuvette in Figure 3A). The resultant yellow supernatant gives red light emission under UV $(365\ \mathrm{nm})$ ) light excitation (right cuvette in Figure 3B). The supernatant has a maximum FL emission at $\dot{6}20\ \mathrm{~nm~}$ (Figure 3C) and gives strong electrochemiluminescence signal in the presence of tripropylamine (TPrA) at potential higher than $+1.0$ V (Figure 3D), accompanied by a significant increase of oxidation current at $+1.05{\mathrm{~V~}}$ (see inset of Figure 3D), which equals to the anodic peak potential of $\mathrm{{Ru}}(\mathrm{{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ .13 These results confirm that the yellow component released from the RuMOFs into water is $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}^{2+}$ . The $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ -triggered release of $\operatorname{Ru}({\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ was observed to be very rapid. The accurate release rate can be obtained by tracking the FL change on a single RuMOF crystal with the inverted FL microscope. Figure 4 shows that ${\dot{\mathrm{Hg}}}^{2+}$ - triggered release of $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)}}_{3}^{2+}$ began at the surfaces of RuMOF crystals (Figure 4A,B), then propagated from outside to inside quickly (Figure 4B,C). During the release process, bright red light emitting crystals were displaced by dark blue FL objects (Figure 4D). The complete release of $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}^{2+}$ could be finished within tens of seconds, depending the concentration of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}.$ , i.e., the higher $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ concentration, the faster $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)}}_{3}{}^{2+}$ release from the MOFs (Figure S7 in the Supporting Information). It seems as if the morphology of the MOFs did not change during the fast release of $\mathrm{{\hat{Ru}}}(\mathrm{b}\bar{\mathrm{py}})_{3}^{~2+}$ since their shapes are almost the same before (Figure 4A) and after (Figure 4D) the release. However, after a long time placing (more than $12\mathrm{h}$ ), the compact MOF crystals deposited at the bottom of cuvette were changed to white floccular objects (Figure S8 in the Supporting Information), indicating the disassembly of RuMOFs or a significant change in morphology. The fast $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ -triggered release can be attributed to porous structure of MOFs, which facilitate the diffusion and interaction of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{{Ru}}(\ensuremath{\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ . It was found that a small amount of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ could trigger the release of a large amount of $\mathrm{{Ru}}(\ensuremath{\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ . Table S1 in the Supporting Information lists the concentration of $\operatorname{Ru}({\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ inductively released from RuMOFs by various concentrations of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ . The data shows that the picomolar level of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ can trigger the release of the micromolar level of $\mathrm{{Ru}}(\ensuremath{\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{\ 2+}$ . The release efficiency, i.e., the molar ratio of $\mathrm{{Ru}}(\mathrm{{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ to $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ was calculated to be in the range from ${\sim}10^{3}$ to ${\sim}10^{6}$ . This means that one $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ ion can inductively release thousands to millions of $\bar{\mathrm{Ru}}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+};$ depending on the concentration of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ used.
Apparently, the $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ -triggered release of $\operatorname{Ru}({\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ from RuMOFs is not simply based on ion-exchange since the release efficiency via ion-exchange equilibrium should be less than 1. Therefore, it was hypothesized that the release of a large amount of $\mathrm{{Ru}}(\ensuremath{\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{2\mp}$ from RuMOFs is associated with the disassembly of RuMOF structure and complete release of $\operatorname{Ru}({\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ therein. The MOF disassembly hypothesis was confirmed by XRD, BET, TEM, and SEM experiments. XRD data show that the characteristic XRD peaks of MOFs (curve a in Figure 5A) completely disappear after the interaction of RuMOFs with $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ (curve b in Figure 5A), an indication of destruction of RuMOFs. The XRD peaks of the resultant solid (i.e., after $\mathrm{{Ru}}(\mathrm{{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ release, curve b in Figure 5A) are the same as those of BPDC (curve c in Figure 5A), indicating that only one component of the MOFs, i.e., BPDC (low solubility in water), remains in the solid phase after disassembly of RuMOFs. BET data shows that resultant solid has very low BET surface area, only ${\sim}11\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (curve b in Figure 5B), implying that the porous structure of MOFs with BET surface area of $\overline{{424}}\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (curve a in Figure 5B) is destroyed. TEM images show that the monolithic, uniform, and porous nanostructures of RuMOFs (Figure 5C) were changed into separated and nonuniform nanorods after the release of $\mathrm{{Ra}}(\mathrm{{bpy}})_{3}^{~2+}$ (Figure 5D), an indication of significant morphology change in nanoscale. SEM should show that the regular micrometer-sized rod structure of RuMOFs (Figure 5E) completely collapsed into nanoparticles after the release of the guest molecules (Figure 5F). Apparently, the $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ - triggered disassembly of RuMOF structures results in the release of water-soluble components including $\mathrm{{Ru}}(\ensuremath{\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{2+};$ adenine, and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ into the water phase and leaving the waterinsoluble BPDC in the solid phase.
Mechanism of Disassembly and Release. It is interesting to understand why small quantities of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ ions can trigger the complete disassembly of RuMOFs and the release of large quantities of $\mathrm{{Ru}}(\mathrm{{bpy}})_{3}^{~2+}$ . In structure, the RuMOFs have the same frameworks as their precursor bioMOF-1 (proved by the XRD data), which means that the basis frameworks does not change upon the $\mathrm{{Ru}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ functionalization. However, the BET surface area of RuMOFs is $424\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},$ which is significantly lower than that of bio-MOF-1 $(1388\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ . This means the pores of MOFs are occupied by some $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)}}_{3}{}^{2+}$ ions. Moreover, RuMOFs rapidly collapse in the presence of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ ions (as shown in Figure 4), whereas the bare MOFs (i.e., bio-MOF-1) are very stable upon exposure to $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ .
This suggests that $\mathrm{{Ru}}(\ensuremath{\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ ions not only occupy pores of the MOFs but also dope the frameworks, most likely at the sites of BPDC due to their similarity in molecular size. Apparently, the dopant $\operatorname{Ru}({\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ at the frameworks cannot link zincadenine columns as BPDC does, since all active sites (N atoms) of the bipyridines in $\mathrm{{Ru}}(\ensuremath{\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ combine the central metal ion, $\mathrm{{Ru}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ , thus there is no direct bonding between the bipyridine of $\operatorname{Ru}({\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ and the zinc-adenine columns. However, $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)}}_{3}{}^{2+}$ can be immobilized at the frameworks via electrostatic interactions between positively charged Ru$\left(\mathrm{bpy}\right)_{3}^{2+}$ ions and negatively charged MOFs.19 When $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ is added into the solution, it can be strongly and selectively complexed by the adenine24−26 and thus increase positive charges near $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}^{2+}.$ , which may remove $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}^{2+}$ from the framework by electrostatic repulsion. Subsequently, the removal of the large species (i.e., $\mathrm{Ra}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}$ ions) lead to the collapse of the MOFs and release large quantities of $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)}}_{3}^{2+}$ in the adjacent pores. The disassembly of outside layer of RuMOFs exposes the inner part to $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ and causes further collapse of the inner framework until the RuMOFs are completely decomposed (Figure 6). Other metal ions, such as $\hat{Z}\mathbf{n}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{\dot{C}d^{2+}}$ , $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ and so on, might have much weaker complexation with adenine than $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+24}$ thus their weak ion exchanges with $\operatorname{Ru}({\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ at low metal ion concentrations (e.g., less than $100~\mu\mathrm{M}$ as shown in Figure 2) could not be enhanced by the way of metal ion-adenine complexation and could not cause the collapse of RuMOFs.
Sensing Applications. The excellent ion-selective, facile disassembly, and effective release property of RuMOFs may enable the development of signal-on and signal-amplifying sensing method for the detection of ultratrace $\bar{\mathrm{H}}\mathrm{g}^{2+}$ ions. The excellent selectivity (as indicated by Figure 2) makes it possible to sense $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ in the presence of large quantities of other metal ions without using any separation techniques or masking reagents. The large release efficiencies (Table S1 in the Supporting Information), i.e., each $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ inducing the release of $10^{3}{-}10^{6}~\mathrm{\Ru(bpy)_{3}}^{2+}$ ions, combined with the sensitive fluorescence and electrochemiluminescence detection (Figures S9 and S10 in the Supporting Information) abilities for $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)_{3}}^{2+13,14}}$ makes it possible to detect ultratrace $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ (Figure S11 in the Supporting Information). In practice, the fluorescence sensor for $\mathrm{\bar{H}g^{2+}}$ based on the RuMOFs functional material works well when the concentration of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ is in the range from $25\ \mathrm{pM}$ to $50~\mathrm{nM}_{,\cdot}$ , with a limit of detection (LOD) of $8.2\ \mathrm{pM};$ while the electrochemiluminescence sensor for $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ based on the same RuMOFs is validated in the $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ concentration range from 1 to $100\ \mathrm{pM},$ , with a LOD of 0.53 $\mathsf{p M}$ . The LODs are much lower than the United States Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) mandate of 2 ppb (10 nM) for $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ .23 These LODs also suggest that the fluorescence and electrochemiluminesccence sensors based on RuMOFs are one of the most sensitive optical sensors for $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}.$ at least 3 to 4 orders of magnitude better than those of previously reported optical detection methods (usually with LODs of nanomolar levels).27−29 The applicability of this sensing system for detecting $\mathrm{\stackrel{~\displaystyle~H~g^{2+}~}{~\displaystyle~}}$ in a real sample was further evaluated. We applied a standard working curve method to detect the concentration of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ in the sample of Min River (the largest river in Fujian Province, China). The concentration of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ ions in the Min River sample $\left(n=11\right)$ ) detected using this new approach was $2.49\pm0.075\mathrm{nM}$ ; this value is consistent with the result obtained by the ICPMS method, namely, $2.46\pm$
$0.059\ \mathrm{nM}.$ These results confirm the validity of this sensing method for the detection of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ in real samples.
## CONCLUSIONS
For the first time we synthesized novel luminescencefunctionalized MOFs (i.e., RuMOFs) that could be ionresponsively disassembled and release large quantities of luminescent guest materials. In the RuMOFs, the important luminescent cathodic molecules, i.e., $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)_{3}}^{2+},$ , were both encapsulated in the pores and doped in the frameworks. In aqueous solutions, the RuMOFs were very stable but can be selectively and sensitively disassembled by $\dot{\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ ions, leading to the release of large quantities of $\mathrm{Ru(b\bar{p}y)}_{3}^{2+}$ . The ionresponsive disassembly and release has been proposed to follow the mechanism: certain amounts of $\mathrm{{Ru}}(\ensuremath{\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ molecules are doped in the frameworks by displacing the BPDC linkers with the similar molecular diameter, while other $\operatorname{Ru}({\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ molecules are encapsulated in the pores of MOFs. Upon exposure to $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ , the water-soluble cathodic $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)}}_{3}^{2+}$ molecules are selectively exchanged into aqueous solution, leading to the collapse of the frameworks and release of large quantities of $\mathrm{{Ru}}(\mathrm{{bp}\bar{y})_{3}}^{2+}$ in the pores of MOFs. One $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ can triggered the release of ${\sim}10^{3}$ to ${\sim}10^{6}~\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}$ molecules. On the basis of the ion-responsive release, an ultrasensitive sensing method for $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ has been developed with a very low LOD $\mathbf{\bar{(5.3\times10^{-13}M)}}$ . It can be envisioned that the RuMOFs and similar target-responsive functional MOF materials would have promising applications in ultrasensitive and highly selective chemical sensing and even in accurately controllable drug delivering and releasing.
