Dependence of $\operatorname{Ru}({\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ content (weight percentage) in RuMOFs on the initial concentration of $\mathrm{Ru(\bar{b}p y)}_{3}{}^{2+}$ in the synthesis solution (Figure S1); SEM images of RuMOFs (Figure S2); $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption and desorption isotherms of bioMOF-1 (Figure S3); thermogravimetric analysis of RuMOFs and bio-MOF-1 (Figure S4); photos taken for the interactions between RuMOFs and various anions and methylmercury ion in water (Figures S5 and S6, respectively); fluorescence microscopic images obtained during $\bar{\mathrm{Ru}}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}$ release from RuMOFs at different $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ concentrations (Figure S7); photos taken for RuMOFs $12\mathrm{~h~}$ after adding pure water and $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ solution (Figure S8); amounts of $\mathrm{{Ru(\bar{b}p y)_{3}}}^{2+}$ released from RuMOFs in the presence of various concentrations of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ and induced release efficiencies (Table S1); standard working curves for fluorescence and electrochemiluminescence determination of $\mathrm{{Ru}}(\ensuremath{\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ in aqueous solution (Figures S9 and S10, respectively); and calibration plots of ECL intensity vs $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ concentration (A) and FL intensity vs $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ concentration (B) (Figure S11). This material is available free of charge via the Internet at http://pubs.acs.org.
